nl80211_create_bridge(): mark a newly enslaved interface operationally
up, so the bridge actually forwards on that port.

This is the same defect patch 0011 fixed for the mesh STA, on the AP
side. It only became reachable once patch 0013 stopped the bogus OVS
path from silently no-oping every enslavement -- before that the AP VAPs
were never in a Linux bridge at all, so the port state never mattered.

A Linux bridge holds any port whose netdev operstate is IF_OPER_DORMANT
in the non-forwarding "disabled" state. mac80211_hwsim leaves AP
interfaces dormant (and NO-CARRIER) even while the BSS is up and
beaconing, and nothing in wifi_hal or hostapd sets IFLA_OPERSTATE for
them, so every fronthaul and backhaul AP ended up as:

  wifi1    dormant   ->  bridge port state disabled
  wifi1.1  dormant   ->  bridge port state disabled
  wifi1.2  dormant   ->  bridge port state disabled
  eth1     up        ->  bridge port state forwarding

hostapd is configured with bridge=brlan0, so it opens its EAPOL socket
on the bridge. Frames it sends still reach the air, but EAPOL coming
back from the station is dropped on ingress at the disabled port, so the
4-Way Handshake never advances past message 1 and every association
fails identically:

  client: WPA: RX message 1 of 4-Way Handshake from 02:00:00:0e:d7:22
  client: WPA: RX message 1 of 4-Way Handshake from 02:00:00:0e:d7:22   (retry)
  client: CTRL-EVENT-DISCONNECTED bssid=02:00:00:0e:d7:22 reason=15
  client: WPA: 4-Way Handshake failed - pre-shared key may be incorrect
  ctrl:   wifi_drv_hapd_send_eapol: eapol_timeout callback is called
  ctrl:   wifi_drv_sta_deauth: Enter <sta> 15

reason 15 being 4-Way Handshake Timeout. The supplicant's "pre-shared
key may be incorrect" is a red herring -- it is what wpa_supplicant
always reports when M2/M3 never complete. Verified not to be a key or
suite problem: the AP advertises [WPA2-PSK-CCMP] in a scan, and
Device.WiFi.AccessPoint.N.Security.KeyPassphrase matches what the client
was configured with exactly.

Reproduced with a plain wpa_supplicant client on a separate hwsim radio,
not just with the EasyMesh mesh STA, which is what established this is
an AP-side bug rather than anything mesh-specific.

Setting the port operationally up at the point of enslavement is
necessary but not sufficient for an AP VAP: bringing the BSS up
afterwards puts the netdev back into IF_OPER_DORMANT, which parks the
port in "disabled" again. Observed directly on a freshly deployed
controller carrying only the enslave-time half of this change -- wifi1
came up "up"/forwarding while wifi1.1 and wifi1.2, whose BSSes started
later, were back to dormant/disabled. So re-assert it in start_bss()
once hostapd_setup_bss_internal() has succeeded, which also requires
exporting the helper (it was static) and declaring it in
wifi_hal_priv.h alongside nl80211_create_bridge().

Verified against a live controller: forcing IFLA_OPERSTATE up on the
backhaul AP by hand made the previously-failing 4-Way Handshake complete
end to end --

  sending eapol m1 ... to:02:00:00:d8:b7:19    received eapol m2
  sending eapol m3                             received eapol m4

Not hwsim-gated: a dormant port is non-forwarding on any kernel, so this
is correct anywhere.

---
 wifi_hal_nl80211.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
@@ -87,6 +87,15 @@
 /* ovs-vsctl's compiled-in default database socket. Presence of the kernel module
  * alone does not mean this system's bridges are OVS bridges -- see nl80211_create_bridge(). */
 #define OVS_DB_SOCK "/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock"
+
+/* RFC2863 operational states. nl80211_create_bridge() below needs the helper, which is
+ * defined further down this file, so declare it here. */
+#ifndef IF_OPER_DORMANT
+#define IF_OPER_DORMANT 5
+#endif
+#ifndef IF_OPER_UP
+#define IF_OPER_UP 6
+#endif
 #define ONEWIFI_TESTSUITE_TMPFILE "/tmp/onewifi_testsuite_configured"
 #define KEY_MGMT_SAE_EXT 67108864
 #define MAX_MBSSID_INTERFACES 8
@@ -4488,6 +4497,11 @@
         return -1;
     }
 
+    /* The interface is now a bridge port, but the Linux bridge holds any port whose
+     * netdev operstate is IF_OPER_DORMANT in the non-forwarding "disabled" state, so
+     * nothing -- including EAPOL -- crosses it. See nl80211_set_iface_operstate(). */
+    nl80211_set_iface_operstate(if_name, IF_OPER_UP);
+
     wifi_hal_info_print("%s:%d: bridge:%s nl free\n", __func__, __LINE__, br_name);
 
     rtnl_link_put(bridge);
@@ -9058,12 +9072,6 @@
 
 /* IF_OPER_* live in <linux/if.h>, which cannot be included alongside glibc's
  * <net/if.h> without conflicts. Define the two values we need locally. */
-#ifndef IF_OPER_DORMANT
-#define IF_OPER_DORMANT 5
-#endif
-#ifndef IF_OPER_UP
-#define IF_OPER_UP 6
-#endif
 
 /* Set an interface's RFC2863 operational state via RTM_SETLINK/IFLA_OPERSTATE.
  *
@@ -9073,7 +9081,7 @@
  * connected station is left in IF_OPER_DORMANT forever. The Linux bridge keeps a
  * dormant port in the "disabled" (non-forwarding) state, which silently breaks all
  * bridged traffic over a 4-address/WDS backhaul link. */
-static int nl80211_set_iface_operstate(const char *ifname, unsigned char operstate)
+int nl80211_set_iface_operstate(const char *ifname, unsigned char operstate)
 {
     struct {
         struct nlmsghdr nh;
--- a/wifi_hal_priv.h
+++ b/wifi_hal_priv.h
@@ -1019,6 +1019,7 @@
 int     nl80211_enable_ap(wifi_interface_info_t *interface, bool enable);
 int     nl80211_kick_device(wifi_interface_info_t *interface, mac_address_t mac);
 int     nl80211_create_bridge(const char *if_name, const char *br_name);
+int     nl80211_set_iface_operstate(const char *ifname, unsigned char operstate);
 int     nl80211_remove_from_bridge(const char *if_name);
 int     nl80211_update_interface(wifi_interface_info_t *interface);
 int     nl80211_interface_enable(const char *ifname, bool enable);
--- a/wifi_hal_hostapd.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_hostapd.c
@@ -3422,6 +3422,12 @@
     if (ret != RETURN_OK) {
         wifi_hal_error_print("%s:%d: vap:%s:%d create is failed:%d csa status:%d\n", __func__,
             __LINE__, vap->vap_name, vap->vap_index, ret, interface->u.ap.hapd.csa_in_progress);
+    } else {
+        /* Bringing the BSS up puts the netdev back into IF_OPER_DORMANT, which parks the
+         * interface's bridge port in the non-forwarding "disabled" state again -- setting
+         * operstate at enslave time alone is therefore not enough for an AP VAP. Re-assert
+         * it here, once the BSS is actually running. */
+        nl80211_set_iface_operstate(interface->name, 6 /* IF_OPER_UP */);
     }
 #ifndef CONFIG_GENERIC_MLO
